A||||9||Shiner||Color change and repaint questions||||1||
Z||000000||Shiner||08-28-2000||01:05 AM||||As some of you know, I am finally getting around to replacing body panels and repainting my '87 GT.  Okay, here goes.  Since both the replaced panels and the originals have two coats of paint on them, I was going to get the car bead/soda blasted.  One place around here will do the whole car and spray it with sealer for $350.  Good deal or not?  And will I still have to do a lot of bodywork after this??  <P> Second question:  The car was originally gold, the replacement panels are red and black and silver.  I am going to paint the car red.  Would it be best to leave most of the body panels ON the car, or will I have to take them off so the painter can get to the jambs and hard to reach parts?  I really don't want to pay $1500 for a paint job and see old gold paint when I open the hood.<P> Thanks guys!<P>------------------<BR>Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ralph||08-28-2000||06:14 AM||||I would think you also would not want red overspray on everything. Check out the painter and inspect some of his previous work. If you are up to it, removing panels produces the best results. I have had a local painter here do several panels for me for $75.00 per. No matter how close you look you can not tell there was any repainting.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||08-28-2000||08:15 AM||||The door jambs are black on all Fieros that I know of.  You do have the painted lip inside the engine lid and the front trunk opening to deal with.<P>Also if you are changing colors it would be wise to remove the parting line molding strip and mirrors so they get painted underneath.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-28-2000||10:58 AM||||It's the bead blasting part that makes me nervous...... The paint and rigid pannels are really close in hardness. I'd be real suprised if you didn't dig into the pannel with a blaster.<P>You'd be better to work the surfaces over with a random orbit sander to remove anything that's flaking. You'll have allot more control with the sander.<P>The number of coats isn't as big an issue as the condition of them. If the second coat is peeling from the original that's a problem. It means the second coat wasn't applied right. Nothing can fix that but sanding off the second coat. <P>This is a really big problem with cheap repaints. If the car doesn't get cleaned and scuffed enough before they shoot the paint you end up with wax etc between paint layers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||08-28-2000||02:04 PM||||Ogre,<BR> The gold repaint is flaking in various areas, mostly on the roof.  It was a pretty horrid repaint too.  The thing that worries me is that I can see nice SHINEY paint underneath the second coat.  Do you think even soda blasting would dig into the paint?  I haven't done this kind of body work before on a car, but I am good with my hands and I learn fast, so...  should I just go ahead and sand the freakin thing myself?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||08-28-2000||05:52 PM||||This topic was covered not all that long ago. I'm in the process of painting my GT now. The FIRST thing you want to do before sanding anything, is to wipe it down thoroughly with a product like Prep-Sol to remove wax, oil, and grease. Oterwise you could end up sanding that suff right into the existing paint, which can cause problems with new paint adhesion.<BR>Use a d/a sander with 80 grit paper to rough everything up, then go back over it with 32o to remove the 80 grit sanding marks. Wetsanding is a good idea as well. Any place you go through the paint to the panel material, you'll need to apply a heavy build primer to level it out. <BR>Keep in mind that preperation is the most important element in the paint application process. Crappy prep work will result in a crappy paint job.<P>Good luck-<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000006||jmw||08-28-2000||07:04 PM||||The Forum has been helping me (and others) with similar issues lately. Somewhere along the line I was told that red is the most expensive paint AND the quickest to fade / show damage. Supposedly white is the least expensive / most long-wearing. I don't know if this is true or not, but it may play a part in your decision to switch to red. I wanted to change colors too and was told it would require removing all the panels, whereas if I kept it the same color (mine is red) they could just shoot the car intact. Personally, I am first going to try JUST sanding parts of car (like roof and decklid) myself, and having just those parts repainted - by whom I do not yet know. With sanding, I was told to start with 220, then use 400, then finish up with 600 wet-dry. Someone else said 600 was too rough and recommended finishing with 2000. The car should look very dull and "blackish". I was also told to have whoever is going to do the painting apply the primer, as it is very important that the primer and paint be compatible, and they are not 'interchangeable.' All this info was from Forum folks. Hope I have remembered / represented it correctly, and that it is helpful to you.||||reg||6
Z||000007||rogergarrison||08-28-2000||08:15 PM||||no way id use any blaster or stripper on plastic panels. Just do the orbital sanding, or if you really want ultra straight panels hand sand with a block. Theres never a need to strip off old paint to bare panel unless its been painted many times already. Just get surface smooth, check carefull for pinholes after you prime it, they really show up in finished paint.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||08-28-2000||08:57 PM||||My 2 cents- Only use 80 grit paper on an area that you are going to repair- not for just roughing it up. It will leave very deep scratches in the plastic. On "decent" paint, DA with 240 and wet sand with 400. On good paint, use DA 400 then wet sand with 400. If you go much finer than 400, you will take a chance on not getting proper adhesion. 2000 is more for sanding imperfections out before buffing. If you are going to sand with 2000, just wash the car with a red Scotch Brite pad- the final surface will be about the same. <BR>On areas that need old paint removed, start with 180 grit to strip the paint. It will take a while, but we have small cars. After the 180, 3 coats of primer. Resand with 240 and reprime. Then wet sand with the 400.<BR>With the urethane primers, a lot of shops around here are not even water sanding them- dry sanding with 320, spraying a good sealer and the finish coats. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||08-28-2000||11:33 PM||||Hmm, this ruined my perfect little plan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>  Well, most of the panels on the car have paint that isn't flaking, but the roof and I belive the rear fastback clip are flaking.  So I guess MOST of the car will just have to be roughed up.  It's just the clip and roof that worry me.  I don't have the facilities to sand/prime/sand/prime the car down to perfection, so maybe this will be best left to a competent shop?  I also have a small crack in the rear clip that I will have to have repaired.  <P>  What should I do here?  How much is a shop going to charge to do all this bodywork?||||reg||1
